<TopicPage slug="subjects/sports-science/advanced/periodisation-and-programming">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The training lesson established the engine — stress, recovery, supercompensation. This
    chapter is the transmission: how those engine cycles are sequenced across weeks and months
    so the athlete peaks on a chosen date. Periodisation is that scheduling logic, and writing a
    programme is an exercise in applied physiology: every block of the plan has to answer what
    fitness is being built (the components), how progress will be measured (test protocols), what
    fuel the work runs on (diet and energy systems), and how the load progresses without breaking
    the athlete (the overload principles). The syllabus's whole "health, fitness and training"
    unit is the vocabulary a periodised plan is written in.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the blocks, the models, the taper, monitoring" widget="equationStep" config={periodCfg} />

  <h2>Health, fitness and the relationship between them</h2>
  <p>
    The plan starts from two definitions that are related but not identical.
    <strong>Health</strong> is a state of complete physical, mental and social well-being, not
    merely the absence of disease (the WHO definition) — a property of the person.
    <strong>Fitness</strong> is the ability to meet the demands of the environment — a property
    of a person <em>for a task</em>: a marathon runner is fit for 42 km and unfit for a 1-rep
    maximum, and vice versa for a powerlifter. The relationship is positive but loose: higher
    fitness generally improves health markers (cardiorespiratory fitness is among the strongest
    predictors of all-cause mortality), but you can be fit and unhealthy (an overtrained athlete
    with suppressed immunity and RED-S symptoms), and healthy but unfit (a sedentary person with
    no current disease who cannot climb three flights of stairs without winding). Programming
    must therefore serve both: a good plan builds the target fitness <em>while</em> managing
    sleep, nutrition, stress and load so the athlete's health underwrites the adaptation.
  </p>

  <h2>Components of fitness</h2>
  <p>
    Programmes name their targets precisely, and the standard taxonomy splits in two.
    <strong>Health-related components:</strong> cardiorespiratory endurance (the heart-lung
    system's ability to deliver oxygen — measured as VO<sub>2</sub>max), muscular strength
    (maximum force), muscular endurance (repeated or sustained force), flexibility (range of
    motion at a joint), and body composition (the fat-to-lean ratio).
    <strong>Skill-related components:</strong> speed, power (force × velocity — strength
    expressed fast), agility (change direction quickly under control), balance, coordination and
    reaction time. Every sport demands a different weighting, and that weighting is what makes
    periodisation necessary: a rugby prop and a 1,500 m runner both "train", but they are
    sequencing very different components — and no block can maximise all of them at once, because
    the adaptations partially conflict (large endurance volumes blunt strength gains — the
    interference effect).
  </p>

  <h2>Test protocols and why we test</h2>
  <p>
    You cannot periodise what you cannot measure. <strong>Reasons for testing:</strong>
    establishing baselines, identifying weaknesses to target in the next block, monitoring
    whether the programme is working, setting goals and motivating the athlete, and talent
    identification. <strong>Protocols</strong> map onto components: the multi-stage fitness test
    (beep test) or a lab VO<sub>2</sub>max test for cardiorespiratory endurance; the 1RM or its
    estimated cousin for strength; the vertical jump (Sargent test) for leg power; the sit-and-reach
    for flexibility; skinfolds or DXA for body composition; the T-test or Illinois run for
    agility. Two properties decide whether a test is worth doing. <strong>Validity:</strong>
    does it measure the component in this sport's context (a beep test predicts football fitness
    far better than it predicts rowing fitness)? <strong>Reliability:</strong> does it give the
    same answer twice — same protocol, same warm-up, same tester, same time of day? A worked
    example of testing <em>inside</em> programming: the 1RM is rarely maxed in training; instead
    estimate it from submaximal work with the Epley formula, 1RM = w × (1 + reps/30). An athlete
    squatting 100 kg for 8 clean reps has an estimated 1RM of 100 × (1 + 8/30) ≈ 127 kg, so the
    next block prescribes strength work at 80–90% of 127 kg — roughly 100–114 kg for low-rep
    sets — and the test is repeated at the block's end to confirm the percentage actually
    progressed.
  </p>

  <h2>Diet and energy sources: fuelling the plan</h2>
  <p>
    The plan's sessions burn fuel through three energy systems, and the diet supports whichever
    dominates. The <strong>ATP-PC system</strong> fuels about 10 seconds of maximal effort (a
    sprint, a heavy single); <strong>anaerobic glycolysis</strong> bridges roughly 10 seconds to
    2 minutes, producing lactate as a by-product (not a cause of the burn itself); the
    <strong>aerobic system</strong> carries everything longer, burning carbohydrate and fat with
    oxygen. Macronutrients supply the substrate: carbohydrate and protein yield about 4 kcal per
    gram, fat about 9. Practical numbers: an athlete's muscle glycogen store is roughly 400–500 g
    (about 1,600–2,000 kcal), which a hard 90-minute session can meaningfully deplete — hence
    carbohydrate intake scaled to training load (roughly 3–5 g per kg of body mass on rest days
    up to 8–12 g/kg on heavy endurance days) and protein around 1.4–1.8 g/kg/day for those
    training for strength, spread across meals, since muscle protein synthesis is triggered per
    dose. Periodisation applies to the plate as well as the barbell: fuelling high on high-load
    days and lower on recovery days, and timing carbohydrate around sessions to support the
    quality of the next one.
  </p>

  <h2>Principles of training and overload</h2>
  <p>
    The plan obeys a small set of principles that make the adaptation happen.
    <strong>Progressive overload:</strong> the stimulus must exceed what the body is used to, and
    must keep growing — otherwise adaptation stalls. Load is quantified as volume load
    (sets × reps × kg) for strength work, or duration × intensity for endurance; progression is
    usually applied one variable at a time (add reps before load — the "2-for-2 rule": when the
    athlete exceeds the rep target by two on the last set in two consecutive sessions, raise the
    load). <strong>Specificity:</strong> you get better at what you do — adaptations are specific
    to the energy system, movement pattern and velocity trained. <strong>Reversibility</strong>
    ("use it or lose it"): adaptations decay when the stimulus stops — and they decay at
    different speeds, which the detraining section below quantifies.
    <strong>Individual differences:</strong> the same programme produces widely different
    responses. <strong>Variation</strong> (the FITT variables — frequency, intensity, time, type)
    keeps the stimulus novel and manages monotony. A worked overload cycle for a novice
    presser: 3 × 5 at 40 kg Monday; when all 15 reps are clean, add 2.5 kg; stall, add a
    back-off set, then reset load 10% and rebuild — the plan <em>is</em> the progression rules,
    not the numbers on this week's page.
  </p>

  <h2>Methods of training, sequenced</h2>
  <p>
    The methods are the ingredients the blocks combine. <strong>Continuous training</strong>
    (steady 20+ minutes, aerobic base), <strong>interval training</strong> (work/rest ratios
    targeting specific systems — 1:3 for speed, 1:1 or 2:1 for aerobic power), <strong>Fartlek</strong>
    (continuous running with surges — endurance with a speed flavour), <strong>resistance
    training</strong> (strength and hypertrophy: typically 1–5 reps at heavy loads for strength,
    6–12 for hypertrophy, 12+ for endurance), <strong>plyometrics</strong> (the stretch-shortening
    cycle — jumping and bounding — for power), and <strong>circuit training</strong> (stations,
    general conditioning, useful for large groups). Periodisation does not invent these; it
    decides <em>when each one is on the menu</em> — general capacities first, specific ones
    closer to competition, and never everything at once.
  </p>

  <h2>The periodised plan: macro, meso, micro</h2>
  <p>
    The classical structure (Matveyev) nests three cycles. The <strong>macrocycle</strong> is the
    year or season, ending in the peak; the <strong>mesocycle</strong> is a 3–6 week block with
    one dominant target — hypertrophy (high volume, moderate load), then maximal strength (lower
    volume, high load), then power (load converted to velocity), then competition/peak; the
    <strong>microcycle</strong> is the week, arranging sessions so hard and easy days alternate
    (two or three high-intensity stimuli per week, separated by 48 hours or more for the same
    system). The models differ in how they sequence: <strong>linear</strong> moves from high
    volume/low intensity to the reverse across the year (simple, excellent for novices);
    <strong>undulating</strong> varies the target day to day (heavy Monday, explosive Wednesday,
    hypertrophy Friday — realistic for team sports whose fixtures dictate fatigue);
    <strong>block periodisation</strong> concentrates one ability per short block and banks its
    <em>residual effect</em> — adaptations persist for weeks after training stops — so a
    strength block's gains are carried into a speed block rather than trained simultaneously.
    The honest research verdict: when volume and intensity are actually equated, the models
    perform similarly; what matters is that overload, specificity and recovery are all
    programmed, and that the athlete adheres.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Tapering</strong> is the plan's finale and its most counter-intuitive move: to
    arrive fresh, cut <em>volume</em> by roughly 40–60% over the final 1–3 weeks while
    <em>maintaining intensity</em> — a few short, sharp exposures keep the neuromuscular
    machinery primed, while removing the accumulated fatigue. The logic is the fitness-fatigue
    model: performance = fitness − fatigue, and fatigue decays days after its last dose while
    fitness decays over weeks. Meta-analyses of tapering find performance improvements of
    around 2–3% on average — in a final, the difference between medalling and watching.
  </p>

  <h2>Monitoring: the programme's dashboard</h2>
  <p>
    A plan written in advance is a hypothesis; monitoring is how the data corrects it. The
    cheapest and best-validated tool is Foster's session-RPE: load = session RPE (0–10, rated
    ~30 minutes after) × minutes — a 60-minute hard session rated 7 is 420 arbitrary units.
    Rolling these up gives the <strong>acute:chronic workload ratio</strong> (ACWR): this week's
    load divided by the 4-week average. Gabbett's observational work associated ratios of about
    0.8–1.3 with the lowest injury rates and spikes above roughly 1.5 with elevated risk —
    which formalises the coach's folk wisdom that you cannot arrive at a 10,000-unit week
    having done 4,000 for a month. The ratio is a warning light, not physics: it is
    observational, and absolute load still matters (a "safe ratio" of enormous loads is not
    safe). Alongside load sit wellness questionnaires (sleep, mood, soreness), morning heart-rate
    variability, and quick neuromuscular tests — a countermovement jump more than about 5% below
    the athlete's rolling baseline is a classic fatigue flag. The programme is then adjusted:
    swap a hard session for easy, or bank the readiness and push.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "more training is always better training." Adaptation happens
    during recovery, not during the session; the programme's job is to schedule stress the body
    can absorb. The athletes who plateau are usually not undertraining — they are
    under-recovering relative to a plan copied from someone with a different schedule, genetics
    and food budget.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The fitness-fatigue model formalised.</strong> Banister's two-component model
    treats each session's impulse as producing both a positive fitness effect (decay time
    constant around 15–19 days) and a negative fatigue effect (decaying around 1–3 days in the
    original fits); performance is their difference. It is a toy model — parameters vary wildly
    between athletes and it fits better than it predicts — but it earns its keep by explaining
    three programming facts at once: why the taper works, why hard blocks must alternate with
    easy ones even when motivation is high, and why fitness gained early in the year can still
    be visible at the championships while the fatigue that bought it is long gone.
    <strong>Detraining rates</strong> justify in-season maintenance blocks: VO<sub>2</sub>max
    falls measurably within 2–4 weeks of stopping (plasma volume first, then mitochondrial
    density), while maximal strength decays far more slowly — a trained nervous system and
    tendon stiffness persist for months — which is why team sports run strength maintenance
    (one or two heavy sessions a week) and spend their in-season budget on speed and freshness.
    <strong>Open questions:</strong> the ACWR debate (does the ratio <em>cause</em> injury risk
    or merely mark exposure?), the interference effect's mechanisms and dose thresholds, the
    genetics of responder vs non-responder phenotypes (twin studies show heritabilities around
    50% for training responses), and whether real-time biomarker-guided programming outperforms
    a well-coached RPE-and-eyes approach. The honest current answer to the last one is: not yet
    demonstrably.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Matveyev, Fundamentals of Sports Training; Issurin on block periodisation; Gabbett on training loads and the ACWR; Foster's session-RPE method; the training-load and anatomy lessons.</p>
